    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "“We Gods of Time” is a short story that falls within the genres of science fiction, fantasy, and afrofuturism. The narrative explores a not-too-distant future world in which Lauren Jordan contends with the over-policing of her community and her society’s deployment of data surveillance. In the story, she must grapple with a very personal brush with the legal system. In so doing, she encounters a deity who shares with her a god’s perspective of time and gives her the opportunity to wield god-like influence. The story deals with themes of data surveillance, predictive algorithms, digital identity, autonomous vehicles, and autonomous policing. Its central thematic interest, however, arises from the ideas of time travel and narrative manipulation. Time travel is used as an analogy for the ways in which predictive algorithms, powered by machine learning, relocate decision points that impact society. These points of decision-making opportunity distribute god-like power to privileged groups whose narrative sensibilities are then imposed on the masses through software-mediated social infrastructure.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Zazaboi, J. (2024). We Gods of Time: A Creative Exploration of Surveillance Systems and Predictive Algorithms. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/xt792kt5920. https://doi.org/10.25740/xt792kt5920.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
